\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Left:} the mining mechanism as currently witnessed, terminating in \emph  {questions answerable} rather than in an anatomy delivered --- a capability claim should end in what the artifacts let you ask. Solid edges carry certificates: the label names the gate, the count, or the hazard class whose closure the edge depends on. \emph  {The dashed edge is what is not yet witnessed} --- the argument graphs carry premises, warrants and declared holes, which is the shape an adversarial exchange or a retrieval stage requires, but nothing consumes them. The labelled dependencies on the learning path (S3 $\to $ S10 $\to $ S12) are load-bearing rather than decorative: each names a hazard class whose closure that edge requires, and the three failure modes they represent --- aborting on an id family, emitting to stdout only, and measuring a fixture corpus --- are silent, so an unlabelled diagram would show this path as sound whether or not it is. \textbf  {Right:} A13 as a selection diagram. $S$ switches corpus scale (16 papers $\to $ the full field); ``the pipeline completes at field scale'' is identified only if the $S$-dependence can be separated from the pipeline edge. It cannot yet: \textsf  {H20} was a defect whose \emph  {severity} is scale-dependent (a $23\times $ waste here, ${\sim }138$k redundant rebuilds and non-termination at field scale), which is precisely an $S$-to-outcome path that does not pass through the pipeline edge. Scale transport is therefore a derivation obligation, not an extrapolation --- see \S  \ref {sec:prereg}.}}{17}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dag}{{1}{17}{\textbf {Left:} the mining mechanism as currently witnessed, terminating in \emph {questions answerable} rather than in an anatomy delivered --- a capability claim should end in what the artifacts let you ask. Solid edges carry certificates: the label names the gate, the count, or the hazard class whose closure the edge depends on. \emph {The dashed edge is what is not yet witnessed} --- the argument graphs carry premises, warrants and declared holes, which is the shape an adversarial exchange or a retrieval stage requires, but nothing consumes them. The labelled dependencies on the learning path (S3 $\to $ S10 $\to $ S12) are load-bearing rather than decorative: each names a hazard class whose closure that edge requires, and the three failure modes they represent --- aborting on an id family, emitting to stdout only, and measuring a fixture corpus --- are silent, so an unlabelled diagram would show this path as sound whether or not it is. \textbf {Right:} A13 as a selection diagram. $S$ switches corpus scale (16 papers $\to $ the full field); ``the pipeline completes at field scale'' is identified only if the $S$-dependence can be separated from the pipeline edge. It cannot yet: \hz {H20} was a defect whose \emph {severity} is scale-dependent (a $23\times $ waste here, ${\sim }138$k redundant rebuilds and non-termination at field scale), which is precisely an $S$-to-outcome path that does not pass through the pipeline edge. Scale transport is therefore a derivation obligation, not an extrapolation --- see \S \ref {sec:prereg}}{figure.1}{}}
